This is the easy 20% — checking a single `Calendar` (room or attendee) for overlap with a proposed time range:

```java
boolean hasConflict(TimeRange proposed) {
    for (Meeting existing : bookedMeetings) {
        if (existing.getTimeRange().overlaps(proposed)) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

This works fine for one room. The next slides are about what changes when you need this to be true for a room **and** ten attendees simultaneously, and when meetings recur.

A weekly standup shouldn't create thousands of `Meeting` rows years in advance. Instead, `RecurrenceRule` expands into concrete occurrences only for the window a query actually needs:

```java
List<TimeRange> expand(RecurrenceRule rule, TimeRange queryWindow) {
    List<TimeRange> occurrences = new ArrayList<>();
    LocalDateTime cursor = rule.getFirstOccurrenceOnOrAfter(queryWindow.getStart());
    while (cursor.isBefore(queryWindow.getEnd())) {
        occurrences.add(new TimeRange(cursor, cursor.plus(rule.getDuration())));
        cursor = rule.nextOccurrence(cursor);
    }
    return occurrences;
}
```

A query for "next two weeks" only ever materializes a handful of instances, no matter whether the rule has been running for one month or five years.
